#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
#
# btrfs-stall-monitord
#
# Installed by btrfs-setup.sh (CST). Read-only telemetry
# daemon: records why IO stalls happen on this btrfs workstation so that
# `btrfs-stall-report` can answer "was storage the bottleneck, and why?" for
# any past time window, without wrapping the workload in a script.
#
# Design:
#   * One sampling thread that never touches the filesystems it measures:
#     it only reads kernel counters (/proc, /sys, cgroupfs). A separate writer
#     thread appends to the log files, so a write stuck behind a btrfs commit
#     can never freeze sampling.
#   * Probe every second (btrfs commit_stats, tiny), full sample every 5 s.
#   * Episodes: 1 s sampling plus a kernel-stack snapshot of every task in D
#     state. Opened by a kernel PSI trigger on /proc/pressure/io, by a long
#     running btrfs commit, or by several tasks in D state. The D-state count
#     matters: btrfs waits for commits, log syncs, ordered extents and tree
#     locks use wait_event/rwsems, which PSI "io" does NOT count (it only sees
#     io_schedule), so PSI alone would miss exactly the btrfs stalls.
#   * Each D-state task's stack says what it waits for: local storage, a FUSE
#     filesystem (gvfs SMB shares, the document portal: the network, not the
#     disks), btrfs maintenance (fstrim/FITRIM, async discard, scrub,
#     balance/relocation: background housekeeping that legitimately sits in
#     D state for minutes), or something idle-ish (futex, epoll, USB). Only
#     storage waits open episodes or raise the "stuck on IO" alert; a
#     maintenance task is reported only if it stays in D for MAINT_HUNG_SECONDS.
#   * Fedora kernels have no hung-task detector (CONFIG_DETECT_HUNG_TASK is
#     off), so a task in D state for HUNG_SECONDS without progress is reported
#     here instead. Progress = the task ran on a CPU between two samples
#     (/proc/PID/task/TID/schedstat timeslices), except in timed sleeps that
#     wake up without progress (dirty/reclaim throttling, schedule_timeout
#     loops). A database calling fsync ~100 times a second is in D at every
#     sample yet not stuck: an informational "io-busy" event after
#     HUNG_SECONDS, an alert only if it stays so for BUSY_ALERT_SECONDS.
#   * /dev/kmsg is followed for btrfs/device errors.
#   * Storage: one JSON-lines file per day (by record timestamp) in the logs
#     directory, KEEP_DAYS kept; the filesystem's own zstd compression keeps
#     them small, so no gzip pass.
#   * Severe events are queued in the runtime directory and delivered by
#     btrfs-stall-alert.service (btrfs-notify, outside this sandbox).
#
# Record types (field "t"): meta, s (sample), ep (episode), ev (event),
# al (alert). Counters are stored raw (cumulative); the report computes deltas.

